WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:04.480
A little while back, I was having a conversation&nbsp;
with another creator about combo decks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:07.280
and that conversation went something like this:

00:00:07.280 --> 00:00:10.000
“I don’t know that I’d&nbsp;
consider combo an archetype.”

00:00:10.000 --> 00:00:13.840
“What would you consider it, then? What&nbsp;
broader category does it belong to?”

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:18.000
“I don’t know, a finisher? A thing a deck can do?”

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:18.800
Hmmm.

00:00:18.800 --> 00:00:23.040
On a somewhat related note, last year on a&nbsp;
podcast I was ranking archetypes in commander,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.040 --> 00:00:26.960
and I said that I don’t think the reanimator&nbsp;
archetype is one that’s likely to feel good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.960 --> 00:00:31.680
in commander. Down in the comments, there was a&nbsp;
chorus of people pushing back, saying something&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:37.200
to the effect of “but Snail, graveyard decks&nbsp;
are fun! I love my Sevinne’s Reclamation.” This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.320
is interesting to me because I don’t consider&nbsp;
Sevinne’s Rec a reanimator card. A nice little&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:49.040
utility spell for graveyard decks? Yes. A&nbsp;
card played in proper reanimator decks? No.

00:00:49.040 --> 00:00:54.640
That phrase “proper” might begin to elucidate the&nbsp;
issue, though. I’m saying a phrase, and it turns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.640 --> 00:00:59.920
out that my definition of that phrase differs from&nbsp;
a lot of other people’s definition. Let’s go back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.920 --> 00:01:04.960
to the combo example. What do I think about the&nbsp;
statement that combo isn’t an archetype, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.960 --> 00:01:11.360
just a thing some decks do? Well, in the context&nbsp;
of Commander, it’s a pretty reasonable assertion.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.360 --> 00:01:16.160
Combos are a pretty common way of finishing&nbsp;
off opponents in mid to high power EDH games,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:21.600
so trying to categorize them as an archetype feels&nbsp;
a little tricky. Is every deck that wins with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.600 --> 00:01:28.000
combo sometimes a “combo deck”? If not, where&nbsp;
is the line on what constitutes a combo deck?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.000 --> 00:01:32.480
What percentage of games need to be won using&nbsp;
a combo, what amount of deck space dedicated&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.480 --> 00:01:39.440
to pulling off the combo? It’s a messy thing&nbsp;
to try and properly quantify. So, why do I and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:45.200
many other players think of combo as an archetype?&nbsp;
Put simply, it’s because commander inherits a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:52.160
of cultural signifiers from 60 card formats, and&nbsp;
combo is more of a well-defined archetype there.

00:01:52.160 --> 00:01:57.120
In most 20-life 1v1 formats, combos aren’t&nbsp;
actually the best way to close out a game most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.120 --> 00:02:01.760
of the time. This is particularly true for ones&nbsp;
that feature higher-powered interaction, and we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:07.360
can see this in the most popular wincons in Legacy&nbsp;
and Vintage. Vengevine, Glaring Fleshwraker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.360 --> 00:02:12.720
Psychic Frog, White Plume Adventurer—these are&nbsp;
simply extremely efficient creatures rather than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.000
being part of an A+B infinite combo. The reason&nbsp;
for this is simple—it’s just not necessary to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:22.640
use an infinite combo to kill a single 20-health&nbsp;
opponent on a normal timeline, you’d be cracking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.560
eggs with a sledgehammer. For this reason, combo&nbsp;
decks are generally a more specific thing, decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.680
whose gameplan centers around executing a combo&nbsp;
because it offers some advantage in speed, or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.680 --> 00:02:38.480
strengths in some specific matchups. Not all decks&nbsp;
that win with combos would be widely considered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:44.320
combo decks—some might be a control deck that uses&nbsp;
a combo as a compact finisher—but the Venn diagram&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:48.960
is close enough to a circle that these things are&nbsp;
often viewed as one and the same. If you win with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.960 --> 00:02:55.360
a combo, you’re probably playing a combo deck, and&nbsp;
vice versa. This, once again, contrasts with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:55.360 --> 00:02:59.760
commander format, where combos are frequently the&nbsp;
best way to close out games in a wide variety of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:04.800
different decks. And, the more I thought about it,&nbsp;
the more I found myself agreeing with Commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.800 --> 00:03:11.120
Baumi—Combo isn't really an archetype, it's just&nbsp;
a type of finisher, a thing that some decks do.

00:03:11.120 --> 00:03:15.600
This is paralleled when we think about reanimator.&nbsp;
The point of confusion between myself and the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:20.320
YouTube commenters was that when I think of&nbsp;
reanimator, I first and foremost think of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:26.080
archetype as seen in modern or legacy. This is&nbsp;
a very specific type of deck where the goal is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.080 --> 00:03:30.800
to dump a horrifying threat into the graveyard&nbsp;
through an entomb effect or a discard spell, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:36.000
then use a reanimation spell to bring it back&nbsp;
out into play as early as turn 2. The threats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.000 --> 00:03:40.880
used for this are typically either massive value&nbsp;
sources like Griselbrand or oppressive bombs like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:45.920
Archon of Cruelty, and the goal of the deck is&nbsp;
to capitalize on the massive tempo swing of a big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:51.520
threat coming down so early in order to snowball&nbsp;
into a win. As I said in the podcast episode,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:57.360
I don't think this strategy is a very good idea&nbsp;
to replicate in EDH—trying to snowball a turn two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.880
or three threat into a win seems very likely to&nbsp;
result in simply getting pummeled by the table,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.880 --> 00:04:08.000
unless the threat is something so disgustingly&nbsp;
oppressive that it mostly removes the ability&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:08.000 --> 00:04:12.880
of opponents to play the game, a la Jin&nbsp;
Gitaxias or Void Winnower. A deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.880 --> 00:04:17.360
has some strong follow-up options might work,&nbsp;
but the purest versions of the play pattern are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:23.760
likely to either result in pathetic failure or a&nbsp;
generational pubstomp. The vibes are bad. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.760 --> 00:04:29.120
the commenters on that episode weren't thinking&nbsp;
of what I was thinking of about reanimator—they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:34.640
were thinking about the act of reanimation.&nbsp;
Actual reanimator decks don't really exist&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:34.640 --> 00:04:41.280
in EDH due to the reasons I just laid out, so&nbsp;
when EDH players hear "the reanimator archetype"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.280 --> 00:04:46.800
they think about a general graveyard deck that&nbsp;
reanimates things here or there. In other words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.800 --> 00:04:51.520
reanimation as a thing that some decks do.&nbsp;
And I can’t even say they’re wrong for that,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:56.640
because in practice that's basically&nbsp;
all reanimator is within the EDH format.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:01.680
This pattern continues onto other so-called&nbsp;
archetypes. Take control—I have an idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.680 --> 00:05:06.800
what control means to me, various other people&nbsp;
have an idea of what control means to them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:11.040
but when you get into the broader image of it&nbsp;
in the EDH community it mostly comes down to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.040 --> 00:05:15.840
two things—counterspells and board wipes.&nbsp;
If a deck board wipes every turn or two or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.840 --> 00:05:21.040
counters multiple spells a turn cycle, that's&nbsp;
a control deck. Think Baral, Child of Alara,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:26.160
that sort of thing. I also see this whenever I&nbsp;
do deck reviews—I start digging into a deck that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:30.640
somebody has labeled a control deck, and it's&nbsp;
a massive pile of removal and counterspells.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.640 --> 00:05:35.760
Now, unlike reanimator or combo, I do think&nbsp;
control decks meaningfully exist in EDH,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:35.760 --> 00:05:40.720
and they can actually look relatively healthy&nbsp;
within the broader ecosystem. The most prominent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:40.720 --> 00:05:45.840
feature of a control deck, to me, is a multi-card&nbsp;
engine that repeats effects that generate value&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:50.400
or deal with opposing permanents. Flickering&nbsp;
is a common way of facilitating this, as are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:55.600
graveyard synergies that recycle already-played&nbsp;
cards. This sort of engine then helps a deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.600 --> 00:06:00.000
scale into the late game to the point where it's&nbsp;
nigh-unstoppable. In the context of this sort of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:05.680
deck, removing opposing threats is important,&nbsp;
but so is not removing opposing permanents,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.680 --> 00:06:09.760
picking out the threats that can be left on board&nbsp;
safely while the control deck spends its resources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:14.640
building value instead, while still dealing with&nbsp;
the ones that are more immediately problematic.

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:18.880
So, this is my vision of control in EDH,&nbsp;
and I think it's one that has a highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.880 --> 00:06:24.400
dynamic play pattern, both to pilot&nbsp;
and to play against. The problem is,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.400 --> 00:06:28.320
it's about as effective as a massive&nbsp;
pile of board wipes. Wiping the board&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:33.520
in a four player free-for-all format is&nbsp;
just a really, really strong thing to do,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.520 --> 00:06:38.560
and so a lot of players end up building these&nbsp;
board-wipe-tribal monstrosities that constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:38.560 --> 00:06:43.520
flush away all creatures or even all nonland&nbsp;
permanents, thereby removing all the thinking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:48.640
of what permanents can be allowed to stick&nbsp;
around for a bit. Can I honestly say these&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.640 --> 00:06:54.560
aren't control decks? No, I really can't. A&nbsp;
dainty little flicker-archaeomancer pile is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.560 --> 00:07:00.720
just as valid of a control deck in EDH as a board&nbsp;
wipe pile, that's just how the format works. And,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.720 --> 00:07:06.160
"board wipe pile" doesn't even really refer&nbsp;
to a specific set of goals or play patterns.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.160 --> 00:07:10.720
It's just a thing a deck can do—playing&nbsp;
a board wipe—cranked up to its limit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.720 --> 00:07:15.600
and paired with whatever else a player might&nbsp;
feel like doing that works in tandem with that.

00:07:15.600 --> 00:07:20.160
There are plenty more examples of this to be&nbsp;
found. Whereas 60 card aggro decks have cutthroat&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.800
optimized mana curves and very distinct play&nbsp;
patterns, aggro decks in commander are generally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.640
just midrange decks that are stronger earlier in&nbsp;
the game, such is the challenge of playing in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.640 --> 00:07:33.040
three opponent, 40 life format. Some aggro decks&nbsp;
can get around the three opponent issue by having&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:39.280
most of their damage be pings, or get around the&nbsp;
40 life issue by using commander damage or infect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.280 --> 00:07:44.080
but basically none can dodge both of these&nbsp;
challenges, and as a result all of their speeds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.080 --> 00:07:49.520
will be mediated by the need to punch harder&nbsp;
and stick around longer. We can also think about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:54.640
classic archetypes that are borderline disallowed&nbsp;
at most tables—ponza, prison, and discard. In a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.640 --> 00:08:00.720
cutthroat 1v1 format these are fixtures, but in&nbsp;
the social format of EDH they’re endlessly taboo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.720 --> 00:08:04.960
So, this is the general point I’m trying to make&nbsp;
here—the commander format does not have distinct&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.960 --> 00:08:09.520
archetypes in the same way most other formats&nbsp;
do. And actually, the phrase “archetype” has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.520 --> 00:08:13.840
increasingly been phased out in the commander&nbsp;
player lexicon in favor of a different term:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:18.480
“theme”. I’ve kind of noticed this in some ways,&nbsp;
but it’s taken a handful of these run-ins with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:18.480 --> 00:08:24.400
so-called archetypes to really cement things&nbsp;
in my brain. In the year of our lord 2026,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.400 --> 00:08:28.480
commander decks don’t fit into dramatically&nbsp;
different playstyles, but rather different&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.480 --> 00:08:34.400
flavors of existence. They have things that&nbsp;
they do rather than things that they are.

00:08:34.400 --> 00:08:39.600
However, this observation demands a couple&nbsp;
follow-up questions. For one, did commander ever&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:44.880
feature a lot of distinct archetypes? Was it once&nbsp;
holy and pure, only now marred by a decade and a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.880 --> 00:08:50.160
half of precon designs pushing decks in a more&nbsp;
generic direction? In my subjective experience&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.160 --> 00:08:55.280
the answer is yes, but that’s only because&nbsp;
most of my pre-2023 commander experience was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.280 --> 00:08:59.520
playing with a group of ten budget decks that&nbsp;
me and my friends specifically designed to fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:04.640
into different archetypes. Even there the aggro,&nbsp;
control, and combo decks felt a little midrangy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:04.640 --> 00:09:09.280
compared to their 60-card counterparts, but there&nbsp;
was still a distinctness that doesn’t exist as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:09.280 --> 00:09:15.040
much in commander more broadly. Personally I miss&nbsp;
that differentiation a bit, but it’s definitely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.040 --> 00:09:20.000
a reflection of a cool micro-format me and my&nbsp;
friends built more so than it is a reflection&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:25.680
of how commander used to be. At its inception in&nbsp;
the 90s, commander decks were janky and disjointed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:30.320
to the max, a collection of all of the interesting&nbsp;
and wacky cards that didn’t quite fit into any of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:35.680
the 60 card decks of the day. Anybody who’s played&nbsp;
in a chaos draft can tell you, when the cohesion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.680 --> 00:09:42.080
of a card pool drops, the result is that decks&nbsp;
plans get simpler and more generic. To be clear,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:42.080 --> 00:09:46.240
this is a different style of generic than&nbsp;
modern commander decks. These days decks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.240 --> 00:09:51.200
tend to be stronger overall, featuring both a bit&nbsp;
more cohesion and also a lot more card draw and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:56.000
other vegetables to hold things together. It’s&nbsp;
still a form of continuity, though—commander&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.000 --> 00:10:01.680
decks have always been a bit more generic and a&nbsp;
bit less cohesive than their 60-card counterparts.

00:10:01.680 --> 00:10:07.160
The second question worth asking is, is this fact&nbsp;
good? Is the fact that commander differs from 60&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.160 --> 00:10:13.760
card formats in not having conventional archetypes&nbsp;
a problem? For the most part I would say no, it’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.760 --> 00:10:18.720
not a problem. As I said, I enjoy when commander&nbsp;
decks are a bit more distinct from one another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.720 --> 00:10:22.560
when they fill different roles and peak&nbsp;
in power at different points in the game,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.560 --> 00:10:28.320
but commander decks looking a lot like one another&nbsp;
is very much something to be expected. In a 100&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:28.320 --> 00:10:33.200
card singleton deck, there will be a natural&nbsp;
limit to how specific a deck’s gameplan can be,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.200 --> 00:10:39.360
and that will result in more shared attributes&nbsp;
between decks. In a 4 player, 40 life format,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.360 --> 00:10:44.000
everything will be slower, and as a result&nbsp;
decks will be expected to grind at least into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.000 --> 00:10:49.520
the midgame. This also means that almost all&nbsp;
decks will want card draw, ramp, and removal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:54.080
unlike in 60 card formats where some decks are&nbsp;
able to get away with little to none in one or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.080 --> 00:10:59.520
more of these categories. Precons have certainly&nbsp;
prodded commander in a particular direction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:59.520 --> 00:11:04.800
but I don’t think that direction represents&nbsp;
anything aberrant or unexpected. Maybe if I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:09.200
grew up playing the super-duper janky version&nbsp;
of commander I’d be resenting the gentle power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.200 --> 00:11:14.640
creep and gradual streamlining of&nbsp;
precons, but I didn’t so I don’t.

00:11:14.640 --> 00:11:19.520
The one part of this that is a little less ideal&nbsp;
is the fact that non-commander formats have become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.520 --> 00:11:24.560
so secondary within the game. It’s absolutely&nbsp;
not a problem for a more casual format with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.560 --> 00:11:30.320
less clearly differentiated decks to exist, but&nbsp;
what about when it’s the default? I remember back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.320 --> 00:11:35.120
in the early to mid 2010s when commander lived&nbsp;
alongside other formats. Somebody who wanted to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.120 --> 00:11:39.280
play commander with their friends could do so,&nbsp;
since most people had a commander deck or two,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.280 --> 00:11:42.800
but they also wouldn’t have too hard a&nbsp;
time finding somebody who wanted to play&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.800 --> 00:11:48.480
standard or modern or pauper or even kitchen&nbsp;
table 60 card Magic. Contrast that with now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.480 --> 00:11:53.040
where it feels like the average Magic player is&nbsp;
more likely to have a 4th or 5th commander deck&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:53.040 --> 00:11:57.600
than they are to have a 60 card deck put together&nbsp;
and ready to play with, and they probably aren’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.600 --> 00:12:02.320
even interested in playing in a draft. And just&nbsp;
to be clear, there are absolutely reasons that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.320 --> 00:12:06.960
commander has succeeded to the extent it&nbsp;
has, reasons that go beyond the outsized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.960 --> 00:12:12.080
level of support it gets by Wizards of the Coast&nbsp;
compared to other formats. It’s great as a casual,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.080 --> 00:12:16.880
social format. But, it’s worth keeping&nbsp;
in mind what we might be missing out on.

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:20.960
Thanks for watching, and thanks to Archidekt&nbsp;
